Algorithmic Bias: Literature Review on Gender Biased Algorithms of Social Media Platforms
Shengkai WANG, City University of Hong Kong

Introduction
Adding just two words (like "for women" or "for students") can bring significant difference in advertisments impressions compared with neutral advertisements (Cecere et al., 2024). These phenomena are examples of algorithmic bias. Algorithmic bias on social media platforms is a systematical skew of the contents pushed to the users according to the users' previous behaviours on the platform. This can be also affected by how the algorithms are designed (Baer, 2019). It comes from the historical prejudices and the contents previously given to design the algorithms (Mikołajczyk-Bareła & Grochowski, 2023). It is not like education biases or healthcare biases since social media platforms serve billions of people all over the world. A small content may influence millions of people daily. Although the companies behind the social media platforms say that personalised contents can enhance the users' experience when they are using the platforms, they should still prove that their algorithms should not be gender-biased in order not to lead to serious gender problems or social norms. The roadmap of this literature review is firstly examining the harms of gender-biased algorithms, then talking about the counter argument, which is regarded to free speech and marketing events.